3.29.m:
and [meditating upon] Śrī Kṛṣṇa, whose form is light, the primeval source of all, beyond Prakṛti,
meditating, in supreme bliss, with his body bristling with horripilation,
singing aloud with melodious voice and tearful eyes that ocean of virtues;

To his left [stood] Kārttikeya, and to his right Gaṇeśvara;
in front of him [stood] Nandīśvara, Mahākāla, and Vīrabhadra;
on his lap he saw his beloved Gaurī, the daughter of the king of mountains.

3.32.m:
Endowed with the six supernormal knowledges, possessing ten powers, travelling at the speed of thought he surely becomes;
Omniscient and giving everything, he becomes the giver of all fortunes;
He constantly becomes like a wish-fulfilling tree by the grace of Kṛṣṇa.

3.34.m:
by whose command the wind constantly blows, overcome with fear;
death wanders perpetually, and the sun shines constantly;
Indra showers rain, fire burns, and time wanders as if frightened;
all stationary beings stand firm, and moving beings move constantly;
trees blossom in season, bear fruit, and are adorned with sprouts,
and dry up in due course, and grow in due course by his command;
creation itself appears and disappears by his command;
by his command everything occurs, not at all by the own will of men.
The Lord Jāmadagnya of great power is a portion of Nārāyaṇa;
His vow can never become unfulfilled.
I know for certain, O woman of faithful vows: 'I am to be killed by him.'
Knowing all this future, how then can I go for refuge?
For those of established renown, ill fame is worse than death."

3.35.m:
a man bitten by a snake, a serpent, an iguana, a hare, poison,
śrāddha-food, a rice-ball offering, a moṭaka, and sesame seeds;
a temple priest, one who rides a bull, one who eats food from a śūdra's śrāddha,
one who cooks food for a śūdra, a priest for a śūdra, a village priest;
an effigy made of kuśa grass, one who burns corpses,
an empty pot, a broken pot, oil, salt, and bone;
cotton, a tortoise, powder, a barking dog,
and on the right a jackal making a dreadful howl;
a cowrie shell, shaving, cut hair, nails, dirt,
quarrel, lamentation, and people engaging in them;
an inauspicious person weeping, and one crying and causing sorrow;
one who gives false testimony, a thief, a murderer of men,
the husband and son of an unchaste woman, one who eats the food of an unchaste woman;
one who steals the goods and wealth of deities, gurus, and brāhmaṇas,
one who takes back what was given, a robber, a violent man, an informer, a rogue;
one detached from father and mother, a killer of twice-born men and the sacred fig tree,
a destroyer of truth, an ungrateful person, a stealer of deposits;
one who harms brāhmaṇas and friends, a wounded man, a betrayer of trust,
a reviler of gurus, deities, and twice-born men, one who mutilates his own body;
a slayer of living beings, one lacking limbs, a cruel man,
one devoid of vows and fasting, one without initiation, a eunuch;
one whose limbs are afflicted with leprosy, a one-eyed man, a deaf man,
a pulkasa, one whose penis is cut, one drunk on liquor, liquor itself;
a man thrown down, one vomiting blood, a buffalo, a donkey,
urine, feces, phlegm, a dry-skinned man, a human skull;
a fierce gale, a rain of blood, musical instruments, the falling of trees,
a wolf, a boar, a vulture, a hawk, a heron, a bear;
a noose, dry wood, a crow, sulfur,
a brāhmaṇa who accepts forbidden gifts, one who lives by tantras and mantras,
